{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 16, :comments ["<b>And Abraham weighed to Ephron.</b> Defective in its spelling — missing a וי״ו. Ba'al ha-Turim wrote that it is because he was grudging of eye (רַע עַיִן), and עֶפְרֹן defective in its spelling — missing a וי״ו — yields the numerical value רַ״ע עַ״יִן. And the reason for the matter is according to what the Sages said (Bava Batra 9a): whoever gives a coin to a poor person is blessed with six blessings, etc.; and anyone who is grudging of eye toward his brother the pauper and gives him nothing at all is not blessed with these six blessings — therefore the וי״ו of עֶפְרֹן is missing, for on account of his being grudging of eye these six blessings were lacking from him. And the Sages said (Sotah 38b): the cup of blessing is given only to one who is generous of eye (טוֹב עַיִן), as it says (Proverbs 22:9), ״טוֹב עַיִן הוּא יְבֹרָךְ״ — do not read ״יְבֹרָךְ״ (he will be blessed) but rather ״יְבָרֵךְ״ (he will bless). And in any case it shows that both of them are true — that is, the read form (קְרִי) and the written form (כְּתִיב) — for one who is generous of eye blesses others with a favorable eye, and therefore he is also blessed with six blessings, corresponding to the indication of the וי״ו of ״יְבֹרַךְ״ — for that is written plene (מָלֵא)." "And you may know further: for רַע עַיִן yields the numerical value four hundred, and therefore we find in four places the number four hundred in connection with a person of grudging eye: (1) In the case of Ephron, who was grudging of eye — therefore he took four hundred shekels of silver. (2) In the case of Joseph's brothers, whose eye was grudging toward their brother Joseph the righteous, and they were jealous of him on account of the coat of stripes that he wore — through this the matter rolled on and they descended to Egypt and were four hundred years in sojourning, affliction, and servitude. (3) In the case of Esau, whose eye was grudging toward his brother Jacob, and he was jealous of him on account of the blessings with which his father had blessed him — and therefore Jacob said (Genesis 32:17), ״וְרֶוַח תָּשִׂימוּ בֵּין עֵדֶר לְעֵדֶר״, in order to fill the eyes of that wicked man; and therefore Esau came with four hundred men with him. (4) Nabal the Carmelite was a stingy man and grudging of eye, as is known — therefore David came upon him with four hundred men to repay him according to his measure."], :book :genesis, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 23, :notes {0 "The Kli Yakar plays on the defective spelling of עפרן (missing a vav in Gen 23:16), the gematria of רע עין, and the plene/defective spelling of יברך in Prov 22:9; all Hebrew letter-plays and numerics are kept visible."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:29:25.759384508Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}